.TH ML 3
.SH NAME
ml \- machine-learning inference device
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bind -a '#N' /dev
.PP
.B /dev/ml/clone
.br
.BI /dev/ml/ n /ctl
.br
.BI /dev/ml/ n /model
.br
.BI /dev/ml/ n /info
.br
.BI /dev/ml/ n /in
.br
.BI /dev/ml/ n /out
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.I ml
device runs a trained model on whatever inference hardware the host has.
The model is
.SM ONNX ,
which is the point: the same file runs everywhere, and what each host does
with it underneath is the host's business.
.PP
On Apple silicon that underneath is
.I onnxruntime
handing the parts of the graph it can to CoreML, and so in practice to the
Neural Engine.
macOS cannot do this in a system framework \(em CoreML compiles
.B .mlmodel
and
.B .mlpackage
and nothing else, and Apple's own Python converter dropped
.SM ONNX
\(em so the conversion happens inside
.I onnxruntime
rather than in the operating system.
.PP
Where the library is not linked the device exists but reports that it has no
backend.
.PP
Opening
.B /dev/ml/clone
allocates an instance and turns that same file descriptor into the
instance's
.BR ctl ,
exactly as
.IR draw (3)'s
.B /dev/draw/new
does.
Reading it gives the instance number
.IR n ,
whose directory holds the rest of the files.
Closing the
.B ctl
file releases the instance and everything it holds; closing
.B in
or
.B out
does not, so a program may open, write and close them as often as it
likes.
.PP
An instance's files may be held open after its
.B ctl
file is closed, but they will not then work: the instance is gone, and
the slot it used may belong to another one, so such a file descriptor is
refused with
.B "file does not exist"
rather than being retargeted.
.PP
An instance is a directory rather than a single request/response file
like
.IR gpu (3)'s,
because a model has four genuinely different things to say: bulk
write-once bytes, a read-only description, an input and an output.
Carrying those on one file means inventing a framing protocol, which is
what
.IR gpu (3)
had to do and why it is not the template for anything larger.
.SS Loading a model
Write the model to
.BR model ,
in as many writes as convenient \(em they are appended, not positional
\(em then write
.B load
to
.BR ctl .
.PP
The model crosses as
.IR bytes .
No host path is ever handed over.
An Inferno path is not a host path: it may name a file on a
.IR styx (10)
mount from another machine, or one that exists only in the calling
process's namespace, so handing it to a host framework would puncture
the namespace for nothing.
This is not a compromise forced by the host \(em CoreML compiles a
staged single-file model and produces the compiled form itself.
.SS Finding out what it takes
.B Info
becomes readable once a model is loaded, and reading it is not optional:
without it a client cannot form a valid write to
.BR in .
Its lines are
.IP
.EX
in \fIname type dim\fP...
out \fIname type dim\fP...
device \fIwhat ran it\fP
.EE
.PP
where
.I type
is
.BR f64 ,
.BR f32 ,
.B f16
or
.BR i32 .
Do not assume
.BR f32 :
a model that declares
.B f64
needs twice the bytes, and a client that guesses wrong gets a
plausible-looking wrong answer rather than an error.
.PP
The
.B device
line says what
.I actually
ran the model, not what was asked for.
Compute units are a request the host may decline, and reporting a
request as though it were a result is the mistake
.IR gpu (3)
shipped once, claiming
.B f64
on a wire that carried
.BR f32 .
Where the host will not say, the line says so rather than echoing the
request back.
.SS "Dimensions a model leaves open"
A model may declare a dimension it does not fix, and
.B info
reports that as
.BR -1 .
A sequence model usually leaves two: how many sequences, and how long they
are.
.PP
One open dimension needs nothing said about it \(em the amount written decides
it.
Two cannot be worked out from a byte count, so the client says:
.IP
.EX
feed input
shape 1 15
.EE
.PP
for one sequence of fifteen.
.B Shape
applies to the input
.B feed
selected, and must follow
.BR load ,
since until then there is nothing to check it against.
It cannot contradict a dimension the model fixed.
.PP
Guessing instead would be inventing an answer, and the answer would be a
plausible-looking wrong one.
.SS Running it
Write the input to
.BR in ,
write
.B run
to
.BR ctl ,
then read
.BR out .
.PP
.B In
and
.B out
carry raw elements of the type
.B info
gives,
.IR big-endian .
That is the same wire
.IR gpu (3)
uses and what
.IR math (2)'s
.B export_real
and
.B export_real32
already produce, so a Limbo caller marshals with what it has; it also
means a model reached across a
.IR styx (10)
mount from a machine of the other byte order still receives the bytes it
meant.
Converting to whatever the host wants happens on the host side, which is
the end that knows the element type.
.PP
A write to
.B in
whose length does not match the feature's declared shape and type is
refused, with an error naming both sizes.
.SS ctl
.TP
.BI units " cpu|gpu|ane|all"
Which compute units to ask for.
This is part of the configuration a model is loaded with, so set it
before
.BR load ;
what actually resulted is on
.BR info 's
.B device
line.
.TP
.BI feed " name"
Which input subsequent writes to
.B in
fill.
Needed only for a model with more than one input.
.TP
.BI take " name"
Which output subsequent reads of
.B out
drain.
Needed only for a model with more than one output.
.TP
.B load
Compile and load the bytes written to
.BR model .
.TP
.B run
Run the model and fetch its output.
.TP
.B reset
Discard the model and start over.
.SH SOURCE
.B /emu/port/devml.c
.br
.B /emu/MacOSX/win-ml.m
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR mltest (1),
.IR gpu (3),
.IR draw (3),
.IR math (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Errors from the host's inference framework are passed through, so a
malformed model or a wrong input size reports what the framework said
rather than a generic failure.
.SH "THE LIBRARY"
.I Onnxruntime
is fetched, not vendored: 41MB of third-party dylib has no business in a
source tree, and it is twenty times the size of the emulator that links it.
.PP
Take the macOS release from
.B github.com/microsoft/onnxruntime
and put its
.B lib
and
.B include
where the build looks:
.IP
.EX
cp lib/libonnxruntime.*.dylib $ROOT/MacOSX/arm64/lib/
cd $ROOT/MacOSX/arm64/lib
ln -s libonnxruntime.*.dylib libonnxruntime.1.dylib
ln -s libonnxruntime.*.dylib libonnxruntime.dylib
cp include/*.h $ROOT/MacOSX/arm64/include/onnxruntime/
.EE
.PP
Both links are needed and the first is easy to miss: the dylib's install name
is
.BR @rpath/libonnxruntime.1.dylib ,
so a build that links against
.B libonnxruntime.dylib
alone will link and then fail to start.
.SH BUGS
This runs a compiled model graph, and that is all it does.
The Neural Engine has no public interface outside CoreML, so
.I ml
will not accelerate a
.IR krylov (2)
solve or a
.IR pde (2)
stencil sweep, and it is not a general compute device.
It is worth having for inference and for nothing else.
.PP
Only tensors are handled.
A model taking a map or a sequence reports that feature as
.B opaque
in
.B info
and cannot be fed.
.PP
A model may leave at most one dimension of an input open.
Two cannot be worked out from the amount written, and guessing which to grow
would be inventing an answer, so such a model is refused rather than fed
something plausible.
.PP
There is no way to enumerate the compute units the host has, only to ask
for one and read back what happened.
.PP
The whole model is held in memory before it is compiled, and again by
the host afterwards.
